app{version}{build}{opensslversion}{curlversion}	App: {0} ({1}), OpenSSL: {2}, curl: {3}
guidance_warning	If you are seeing this warning while inspecting the website for social media, banks, personal email, then your device’s security may be compromised. Avoid using your device if you can and seek immediate assistance from Apple Support or a trusted friend.
reminder_permission_message	TLS Inspector requires permissions to your reminders to add a certificate expiry reminder. You can update permissions from the Settings app.
whatDoesThisMean::badAuthority	This means that the certificate presented by the remote server was issued by a known untrustworthy & dangerous issuer or authority.
whatDoesThisMean::invalidDate	This means that the certificate used by the remote host is outside of its validity period. All certificates must have a date range for when they are valid, and this certificate is currently outside of that range.
whatDoesThisMean::issueDateTooLong	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote server are invalid.\n\nSpecifically, a certificate has too long of a lifetime. All internet certificates must define a date range for which they are valid, and your device has established a maximum for that range. This certificate exceeds that maximum range.
whatDoesThisMean::leafMissingRequiredKeyUsage	This means that the certificate presented by the remote server is invalid.\n\nSpecifically, it is missing required information about what that certificate can be used for. Internet certificates can only be used for specific tasks, and this certificate is missing the required tasks in what it’s allowed to be used for.
whatDoesThisMean::locallyTrusted	This means that your device has been configured to trust this connection even though it would not have done so normally.\n\nEnterprise or corporate devices may use these types of certificates for internal or private websites, but they can also be an indication of a compromised device or network.
whatDoesThisMean::revokedIntermediate	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote host have been marked as “revoked”. Certificates are revoked when they can no longer be trusted due to several reasons.
whatDoesThisMean::revokedLeaf	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote host have been marked as “revoked”. Certificates are revoked when they can no longer be trusted due to several reasons.
whatDoesThisMean::selfSigned	This means that the certificates presented by the remote host were not issued by a trusted certificate issuer or authority. Internet certificates are only trusted by your device if they were issued by responsible and reputable authorities.
whatDoesThisMean::sha1Intermediate	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote host uses the SHA-1 algorithm. This algorithm was widely used but has been found to be vulnerable and is no longer considered secure.
whatDoesThisMean::sha1Leaf	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote host uses the SHA-1 algorithm. This algorithm was widely used but has been found to be vulnerable and is no longer considered secure.
whatDoesThisMean::trusted	This means that your device was able to establish a secure and trusted connection to the host.
whatDoesThisMean::unknownCriticalExtension	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote server are invalid.\n\nSpecifically, they contain information that is unknown to TLS Inspector but is marked as being critical. In this instance, the certificate cannot be trusted as TLS Inspector cannot process information that has been marked as critical.
whatDoesThisMean::untrusted	This means that your device was unable to establish a secure connection or did not have confidence in the identity of the remote host.
whatDoesThisMean::weakRSAKey	This means that one or more certificates presented by the remote host are using weak cryptography. Your device needs to be able to establish a secure connection to the remote host, but the remote host has requested that your device use insecure settings for that connection.
whatDoesThisMean::wrongHost	This means that the certificate used by the remote host is not intended for the host you’ve connected to. Internet certificates only apply to specific domain names or IP addresses, and this certificate does not contain any values that match the host you’ve connected to.
